---
name: vulkan-mapped-arena-reads
description: On this Strix Halo box the host reads a write-combined Vulkan buffer at 0.18 GB/s and a HOST_CACHED one at ~25; always check the memory type before blaming a kernel.
metadata:
  type: project
---

Measured 2026-09-16 (research/llm-vertical.md L6b, `results/l6b_arena.csv`,
`go test ./llm/ -run TestArenaMemoryTypes`): a mapped `VkBuffer` on this
machine is written at ~50 GB/s and **read at 0.18 GB/s** when it comes from a
write-combined memory type — which is what a plain "prefer DEVICE_LOCAL |
HOST_VISIBLE" search picks. Type 5, `HOST_VISIBLE|HOST_COHERENT|HOST_CACHED`,
reads the same buffer at **~25 GB/s** and writes it at ~70. The *device* reads
every type at 236-237 GB/s (research/llm-vertical.md L0b), so the choice is free on the GPU.

**Why:** in the LLM vertical this single `memoryTypeIndex` was **5.0x on the
whole graph** — 280 tok/s to 1400-equivalent — and it looked exactly like a
slow kernel until the read rate was measured. `vk.Device.NewHostCachedBuffer`
and `llm/arena.go`'s `newArena` exist because of it; `LLM_ARENA_UNCACHED=1` is
the control.

**How to apply:** any time host wall clock around GPU work is far above the
GPU timestamps, price a read of the arena before touching a shader. Use the
cached type for activation arenas the host reads; leave weight banks alone,
since they are written once and never read. Applies to the other verticals'
arenas too (`zimage/`, `parakeet/`) — none of them have been checked yet.

Two more host-side traps found the same way (research/llm-vertical.md L6c, 2026-09-16). **Never
cross a block boundary through the host when a dispatch can do it** —
`shaders/llm_move.comp` + `llm/move.go` is the pattern, one pipeline per
(source, destination) buffer pair, and it is bit-exact because SPIR-V's
`float16_t(v)` and Go's `F32ToF16(v)` round identically. And
**`buf.WriteFloat32At(off, make([]float32, n))` is two costs**, the allocation
usually the larger: use `vk.Buffer.ZeroFloat32At` / `ZeroUint16At` / `Zero()`.
A cost that is flat per call shows up as a host column that *falls* with
problem size — that is the signature to look for.
---
name: vulkan-arena-memory-type-is-free
description: "The HOST_CACHED activation arenas cost the GPU nothing even at 3.85 GB — measured with LLM_ARENA_UNCACHED=1 — so the memory type is not hc.cn's 134 GB/s and not the wide-arena decode cost."
metadata: 
  node_type: memory
  type: project
  originSessionId: dedbcc64-a855-44f2-a0fa-3d37d0946061
  modified: 2026-09-22T15:25:27.637Z
---

**Measured 2026-09-22 (P15), and it is a refusal worth not re-deriving.**
`llm/arena.go` puts the activation arenas on a HOST_CACHED memory type because
the host reads them at 24.76 GB/s there against 0.18 on the write-combined
type, and it claims — from L6b — that this is free on the device side. Since
P13/P14 the **KV planes share that buffer**, which is 3.85 GB at 139 000 cells,
so the claim was being made about an allocation two orders larger than the one
it was measured on.

`LLM_ARENA_UNCACHED=1` is the control the file itself defines. Whole model,
48 layers, depth 0 and 128 000:

| | cached | uncached |
|---|---:|---:|
| `hc.cn` (prefill, µs) | 1272.2 | 1260.4 |
| `moe.up` (prefill, µs) | 12170.1 | 12131.0 |
| `dn.qkv` (prefill, µs) | 4265.2 | 4259.4 |
| GPU total, prefill (ms) | 1748.5 | 1744.2 |
| GPU total, decode (ms) | 516.9 | 523.9 |
| **host glue, decode (ms)** | **2.7** | **78.7** |
| decode tok/s | 29.74 | 25.73 |

**Every kernel is within 1%.** The 29x on host glue is the evidence the knob
was really thrown, and it is exactly what `arena.go` predicts.

So: the memory type is **not** `hc.cn`'s 134 GB/s (a third spent hypothesis
after P12-4's two — the 256-way tree and the `gamma` read, both also nothing),
**not** the unexplained 8% that 1.5 GB of unread arenas cost a decode step
(P12-7), and there is **no win available** from moving the KV planes onto their
own memory type. Do not propose it again without a new reason.

What is left for `hc.cn` is the access pattern: four read-modify-write streams
plus an fp16 write a token, where a copy runs two.

**Why:** "the cached type costs the kernels nothing" is the kind of claim that
looks like an obvious suspect every time someone reads the file, and it costs
~10 minutes and the whole machine to re-test. See
[[vulkan-mapped-arena-reads]] for the host-side half, which is the part that
*is* real.
#version 450
#extension GL_EXT_shader_16bit_storage : require
#extension GL_EXT_shader_explicit_arithmetic_types_float16 : require
// MiniMax-H3's modulated pre-norm (VIDEO.md M7): an RMS norm, then the
// block's AdaLN modulation, narrowed into the fp16 arena as a GEMM A operand.
//
//   out = x * rsqrt(mean(x^2) + eps) * a + b
//
// with a = norm.weight * (1 + scale) and b = shift folded on the host, one
// pair of vectors per *run* of rows that share a modulation row (a
// (timestep, modality) pair): the packed sequence is [text | keyframes |
// audio | video], so a forward has at most four runs and each is one
// dispatch of this shader with its own pc.aux0 (a) and pc.aux1 (b), both in
// the fp32 activation arena where the per-step tables are uploaded.
//
// The same shader with a = weight, b = 0 is the token refiner's plain norm.
//
// One workgroup per row; one reduction, since an RMS norm has no mean.
#include "dit_common.glsl"

