---
name: vulkan-mapped-arena-reads
description: On this Strix Halo box the host reads a write-combined Vulkan buffer at 0.18 GB/s and a HOST_CACHED one at ~25; always check the memory type before blaming a kernel.
metadata:
  type: project
---

Measured 2026-09-16 (research/llm-vertical.md L6b, `results/l6b_arena.csv`,
`go test ./llm/ -run TestArenaMemoryTypes`): a mapped `VkBuffer` on this
machine is written at ~50 GB/s and **read at 0.18 GB/s** when it comes from a
write-combined memory type — which is what a plain "prefer DEVICE_LOCAL |
HOST_VISIBLE" search picks. Type 5, `HOST_VISIBLE|HOST_COHERENT|HOST_CACHED`,
reads the same buffer at **~25 GB/s** and writes it at ~70. The *device* reads
every type at 236-237 GB/s (research/llm-vertical.md L0b), so the choice is free on the GPU.

**Why:** in the LLM vertical this single `memoryTypeIndex` was **5.0x on the
whole graph** — 280 tok/s to 1400-equivalent — and it looked exactly like a
slow kernel until the read rate was measured. `vk.Device.NewHostCachedBuffer`
and `llm/arena.go`'s `newArena` exist because of it; `LLM_ARENA_UNCACHED=1` is
the control.

**How to apply:** any time host wall clock around GPU work is far above the
GPU timestamps, price a read of the arena before touching a shader. Use the
cached type for activation arenas the host reads; leave weight banks alone,
since they are written once and never read. Applies to the other verticals'
arenas too (`zimage/`, `parakeet/`) — none of them have been checked yet.

Two more host-side traps found the same way (research/llm-vertical.md L6c, 2026-09-16). **Never
cross a block boundary through the host when a dispatch can do it** —
`shaders/llm_move.comp` + `llm/move.go` is the pattern, one pipeline per
(source, destination) buffer pair, and it is bit-exact because SPIR-V's
`float16_t(v)` and Go's `F32ToF16(v)` round identically. And
**`buf.WriteFloat32At(off, make([]float32, n))` is two costs**, the allocation
usually the larger: use `vk.Buffer.ZeroFloat32At` / `ZeroUint16At` / `Zero()`.
A cost that is flat per call shows up as a host column that *falls* with
problem size — that is the signature to look for.
