**Measured 2026-09-22 (P15), and it is a refusal worth not re-deriving.**
`llm/arena.go` puts the activation arenas on a HOST_CACHED memory type because
the host reads them at 24.76 GB/s there against 0.18 on the write-combined
type, and it claims — from L6b — that this is free on the device side. Since
P13/P14 the **KV planes share that buffer**, which is 3.85 GB at 139 000 cells,
so the claim was being made about an allocation two orders larger than the one
it was measured on.

`LLM_ARENA_UNCACHED=1` is the control the file itself defines. Whole model,
48 layers, depth 0 and 128 000:

| | cached | uncached |
|---|---:|---:|
| `hc.cn` (prefill, µs) | 1272.2 | 1260.4 |
| `moe.up` (prefill, µs) | 12170.1 | 12131.0 |
| `dn.qkv` (prefill, µs) | 4265.2 | 4259.4 |
| GPU total, prefill (ms) | 1748.5 | 1744.2 |
| GPU total, decode (ms) | 516.9 | 523.9 |
| **host glue, decode (ms)** | **2.7** | **78.7** |
| decode tok/s | 29.74 | 25.73 |

**Every kernel is within 1%.** The 29x on host glue is the evidence the knob
was really thrown, and it is exactly what `arena.go` predicts.

So: the memory type is **not** `hc.cn`'s 134 GB/s (a third spent hypothesis
after P12-4's two — the 256-way tree and the `gamma` read, both also nothing),
**not** the unexplained 8% that 1.5 GB of unread arenas cost a decode step
(P12-7), and there is **no win available** from moving the KV planes onto their
own memory type. Do not propose it again without a new reason.

What is left for `hc.cn` is the access pattern: four read-modify-write streams
plus an fp16 write a token, where a copy runs two.

**Why:** "the cached type costs the kernels nothing" is the kind of claim that
looks like an obvious suspect every time someone reads the file, and it costs
~10 minutes and the whole machine to re-test. See
[[vulkan-mapped-arena-reads]] for the host-side half, which is the part that
*is* real.
